# Pinned from the real red run of 2026-07-29 with the injection applied.
#
# This is the acceptance criterion "a pull request that breaks the workspace
# suite fails a check", made into an artifact. The injection is an ordinary
# off-by-one in slot substitution — `cursor = r.start` instead of `cursor =
# r.end`, so each placeholder's own text is re-emitted after its value. It is
# deliberately a defect in PRODUCT code rather than in a test: what the lane has
# to catch is a contributor landing a regression, not a contributor editing an
# assertion.
#
# What is being proven is the whole chain, not the test: a broken workspace test
# reaches `scripts/run-tests.sh`, survives the isolation rerun, finds no registry
# entry willing to excuse it, and comes back out as a nonzero exit. That exit is
# the check failing. Before this task nothing in .github/workflows/ ran any of
# it, so the same regression merged green.
exit: 1

# Unregistered, so it may not be waved through. This is the load-bearing line:
# the wrapper's whole purpose is that a red it cannot account for stays red.
contains: NOT IN THE REGISTRY — no entry claims this failure

# Red alone as well as under load. A regression that only appears in a crowded
# suite would be classifiable as a flake; this one is not, and the replay should
# fail loudly if the isolation rerun ever starts passing.
contains: isolation: FAILED (exit 101)

# Both tests that read a compiled template fall, and the verdict says so with a
# count. If only one falls, the injection is no longer reaching the same code
# path and this proof has gone stale.
contains: REAL (2):
contains: slots::tests::compile_substitutes_values
contains: verdict: RED — 2 real failure(s). This red means something.

# The environment section still reports honestly under a red run — the manifest
# is not something that only appears on green.
contains: environ  : complete — no tool requirement was waived
